    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-trianon">Treaty of Trianon</a> of 1920 cost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> 72% of its historical territory and 58% of its population, and left more than three million ethnic Hungarians outside its borders.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> was a kingdom without royalty: after the Diet dethroned Charles IV in 1921, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miklos-horthy">Miklós Horthy</a> ruled as regent until the Germans overthrew him in 1944.</li><li>As an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> power <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> regained Hungarian-majority lands, and in 1944 Hungarian authorities deported hundreds of thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-concentration-camps">Nazi concentration camps</a>, where most of them died.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The prime minister Gyula Gömbös is said to have coined the word &quot;axis&quot; for the alliance he wanted with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and the two countries used it for their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> axis.</p></aside>
    </section>

