    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> dissolved at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, the Hungarian Democratic Republic and then the Hungarian Soviet Republic were proclaimed in 1918 and 1919. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-kun">Béla Kun</a>'s communist government launched the "Red Terror" and drew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> into a war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. In 1920 anti-communists and monarchists purged communists, leftist intellectuals and others they saw as threats, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, in the "White Terror". After the last Romanian occupation forces left, a coalition of right-wing forces restored Hungary as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> on 29 February 1920.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> would not accept a return of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. Archduke Joseph August, who had declared himself regent, stood down after two weeks. On 1 March 1920 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miklos-horthy">Miklós Horthy</a>, the last commanding admiral of the Austro-Hungarian Navy, was chosen regent, with Sándor Simonyi-Semadam as his first prime minister. In 1921 King Charles IV tried to retake the throne, even marching on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> with rebel troops in October; the army stayed loyal to Horthy, and Charles was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madeira">Madeira</a>. On 6 November 1921 the Diet nullified the Pragmatic Sanction of 1713 and abolished the Habsburgs' rights to the throne.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The loss of agricultural and industrial lands at Trianon brought economic decline, budget deficits and high <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> dependent on farm exports. Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istvan-bethlen">István Bethlen</a>'s government stabilised the currency with large foreign loans and introduced the pengő in 1927, and industry, farming and trade grew for most of the 1920s. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a> and the failure of the Creditanstalt bank in Vienna ended the boom, and from the mid-1930s Hungary's economy came to depend on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s.</p><p>Society was split into two worlds. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> had a standard of living roughly equal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>'s, while most of the countryside was very poor and many peasants could not read; on the Great Hungarian Plain half a million people lived on isolated farms, the tanya, without roads or schools. A small nobility owned most of the land. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 1920 found that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> made up 6% of the population; never regarded as "gentlemen" in a society obsessed with status, they were strongly represented in business and the professions. The education minister Kuno von Klebelsberg built 3,500 rural elementary schools, but admitted that the state could not afford enough schools to end rural illiteracy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>'s army served its policy of revision. In April 1941 Hungary let the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a> cross its territory for the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, joined the operations and annexed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/backa">Bačka</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a> and Muraköz. On 27 June 1941 László Bárdossy declared war on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, and, fearing that German support might turn to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a>, the government sent forces to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-barbarossa">Operation Barbarossa</a>. The support cost the Hungarians dearly: almost the entire Second Army of the Royal Hungarian Army was lost at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-stalingrad">Battle of Stalingrad</a>.</p><p>From 1941 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Romani</a> men were forced into the Labour Service battalions of the Honvéd, where, the historian Katalin Katz noted, they were at the mercy of merciless and often sadistic officers and many died. When Hungary tried to leave the war in 1944, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> invaded in Operation Margarethe on 19 March, meeting little resistance, and in October Operation Panzerfaust removed Horthy. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> in November 1944 and laid siege to it; the city capitulated in February 1945. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debrecen">Debrecen</a> government began forming a new army to fight on the Soviet side, but the volunteers made up only small units, the largest being the Volunteer Regiment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Horthy's rule could be construed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>, though his powers continued those of the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>: he could adjourn or dissolve the Diet at his discretion and appointed the prime minister. The succession was never settled; in January 1942 Parliament made his eldest son István deputy regent, but István was killed in a plane crash that August. István Deák described a rightist country ruled by a small clique of aristocrats, civil servants and army officers, which saw itself as a bulwark against Bolshevism.</p><p>Under Bethlen open voting was brought back outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> and the larger cities. His Party of Unity won repeated elections. The numerus clausus law of 1920, passed under Pál Teleki, limited Jewish university students to 5%; Bethlen relaxed it in 1928, but Gyula Gömbös and Bálint Hóman restored it after antisemitic student demonstrations from 1932. Gömbös moved Hungary towards one-party rule on the Italian and German model, and the "Jewish Laws" first limited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> to 20 percent in a number of professions. In 1944 the pro-Nazi Döme Sztójay legalised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrow-cross-party">Arrow Cross Party</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferenc-szalasi">Ferenc Szálasi</a>, installed in October, ruled as "Leader of the Nation".</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Revision of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-trianon">Treaty of Trianon</a> dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>'s politics and foreign policy, and the resentment it caused has lasted to the present. Hungary signed a treaty of friendship with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> on 5 April 1927, and Gömbös acted as intermediary between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and Italy. Through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-vienna-award">First Vienna Award</a> it regained southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, then occupied the Carpatho-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, and through the Second Vienna Award it gained Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>, before joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis powers</a>. Richard Griffiths argues that its economic dependence on Germany, its antisemitic laws and its part in the killing of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> place it within international fascism.</p><p>From May to June 1944 Hungarian authorities rounded up hundreds of thousands of Jews and sent them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-concentration-camps">Nazi concentration camps</a>, where most died; in northern Transylvania many Jews who had welcomed Hungarian rule in 1940 were deported by Hungarian gendarmes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auschwitz-concentration-camp">Auschwitz-Birkenau</a>. Arrow Cross militias murdered 10,000–15,000 Jews. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Romani people</a> were massacred by the Arrow Cross, forced into over 30 work camps and deported to Dachau, Bergen-Belsen and Ravensbrück. Despite strict censorship, Deák called the era "a golden age of literature and art" in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By early 1944 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> was secretly seeking an armistice with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> learned of it, it occupied the country on 19 March, put Horthy under house arrest and replaced Prime Minister Miklós Kállay with Döme Sztójay, who governed with the aid of a Nazi military governor, Edmund Veesenmayer. In August Horthy removed Sztójay and appointed Géza Lakatos, but when Hungary again tried to leave the war in October the Germans kidnapped Horthy's son, forced Horthy to resign and installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferenc-szalasi">Ferenc Szálasi</a>'s Government of National Unity, which fought on beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a>.</p><p>On 21 December 1944 an Interim Assembly met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debrecen">Debrecen</a> with Soviet approval and elected a counter-government under Béla Miklós, former commander of the Hungarian First Army. It declared war on Germany, concluded an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a> with the Allies, set up People's Tribunals and carried out a land reform in March 1945, under the Allied Control Commission led by Marshal Kliment Voroshilov. Szálasi's government, in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, was disbanded in May 1945. A High National Council served as head of state until the monarchy was formally abolished on 1 February 1946, and the Second Hungarian Republic was soon followed by the communist Hungarian People's Republic.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> of 1920 to 1946 was a monarchy without a king, ruled by the Regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miklos-horthy">Miklós Horthy</a> and a conservative, nationalist and fiercely anti-communist elite. Driven to revise the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-trianon">Treaty of Trianon</a>, it joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a>, regained lost lands, persecuted its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Romani</a> and took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>, before German and then Soviet occupation ended it.</em></p>

